I just saw this, and Bob's answer is "I don't know".

I don't think I have ever actually used a lujvo form for this concept,=20
and I don't know what others have used.  The CLL text does, however, say=20
that the lowest algorithm score is for =E2=80=9Clojbaugri=E2=80=9D and barr=
ing any usage=20
history to the contrary, IMHO, the best scoring word should have a=20
dictionary entry, even if it only points to some word that is preferred=20
in usage.  Someone who knows the algorithm will try to look it up based=20
on the scoring.

Back when I was planning the dictionary, I actually wanted up to three=20
entries: the best scoring one (for someone who knows the algorithm, but=20
wants to check the definition/place structure), the fully-expanded form=20
"lojbybangygirzu" (for someone who doesn't know the rafsi) if it is=20
different, and any form that is preferred in usage to those two.

I understand that the current concept for jbovlaste would make it hard=20
to enforce creating all three entries (though I can imagine a software=20
approach that might be used to generate the algorithmic and fully=20
expanded forms with cross-reference pointers to the word-form which is=20
defined, if they do not already exist for a given word.  This might even=20
be a problem with the current jbovlaste, since I suspect that someone=20
currently could add a different form of a lujvo with a different=20
definition, and that error would not be detected.  (But that isn't a CLL=20
production issue).

